---
// Airbnb booking-flow teardown through Csikszentmihalyi's flow lens.
// Screenshots: captured by Aditya's session on 12 Nov 2026 (mobile web, logged out,
// en-GB, Lisbon, 3 nights, 2 adults). Hosts' and guests' names and photos are blurred.
// Animation is driven by the shared script in src/pages/case-studies/[slug].astro.

const img = (f: string) => `/images/case-studies/airbnb/${f}.jpg`;

type Pin = { top: number; left: number; note: string };
type Shot = { file: string; alt: string; pins: Pin[] };

const keeps: { title: string; condition: string; body: string; shot: Shot }[] = [
  {
    title: 'The price you see is the price you pay',
    condition: 'Clear goals · immediate feedback',
    body: 'From the first page of results, each card shows the total for the whole stay, not a nightly rate that grows at checkout. "Pay €0 today" and "Free cancellation" sit beside it, so the real cost of committing is known before tapping in.',
    shot: {
      file: '02-results-price',
      alt: 'Airbnb search results on mobile showing a listing card with a total price of 258 euros, pay 0 euros today and free cancellation',
      pins: [
        { top: 43.5, left: 35, note: 'Total for 3 nights, taxes included' },
        { top: 47.5, left: 57, note: 'Cost of committing now: €0, and free to cancel' },
      ],
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'Long listings, compressed',
    condition: 'Concentration on the task',
    body: 'The host\'s description runs to several paragraphs. Above it, AI-written "Listing highlights" compress it into three scannable points, and a "Learn more about summaries" link explains where they come from.',
    shot: {
      file: '05-listing-highlights',
      alt: 'Airbnb listing page showing three listing highlights summarised from the listing, with a link to learn more about summaries',
      pins: [
        { top: 23, left: 8, note: 'Three highlights instead of the full description' },
        { top: 68, left: 8, note: 'Says how the summaries are made' },
      ],
    },
  },
  {
    title: '277 reviews, read for you',
    condition: 'Concentration · balance of challenge and skill',
    body: 'Nobody reads 277 reviews. "Guests mention" turns them into topics with counts, such as Walkability and Hospitality, so a guest can check what they care about and go deeper only when they want to.',
    shot: {
      file: '07-guests-mention',
      alt: 'Airbnb listing reviews section with topic chips such as Walkability 44 and Hospitality 210; reviewer names and photos are blurred',
      pins: [{ top: 27, left: 54, note: 'Review topics with mention counts' }],
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'Checkout that keeps the guest in charge',
    condition: 'Sense of control',
    body: 'Checkout is two steps, with a progress bar. Dates and guests can be changed in place, the free-cancellation deadline is a real date, and the guest chooses when to be charged.',
    shot: {
      file: '09-checkout-control',
      alt: 'Airbnb checkout step 1 of 2 showing dates, guests, total price, a free cancellation deadline and a choice of when to pay',
      pins: [
        { top: 46, left: 86, note: 'A date to cancel by, not a policy to decode' },
        { top: 59.5, left: 68, note: 'Choose when to pay' },
        { top: 90, left: 22, note: 'Step 1 of 2' },
      ],
    },
  },
];

const interruptions = [
  { screen: 'Home', items: ['Cookie consent dialog', '"Get the app" banner'], count: 2 },
  { screen: 'Search results', items: [], count: 0 },
  { screen: 'Listing', items: ['Translation notice, full screen', '"Rare find!" pop-up', '"Get the app" banner'], count: 3 },
  { screen: 'Checkout', items: ['"Rare find" label, again'], count: 1 },
];

const matrix = [
  { id: 'A', name: 'Inline translation notice', x: 20, y: 62, kind: 'ship' },
  { id: 'B', name: 'One "Rare find" message, at the decision', x: 32, y: 84, kind: 'test' },
  { id: 'C', name: 'App banner off listing pages', x: 62, y: 50, kind: 'later' },
  { id: 'D', name: 'Cookie dialog', x: 84, y: 22, kind: 'constraint' },
];
---
<article class="story story--airbnb">
  <!-- Cover -->
  <header class="story-cover wrap">
    <div class="story-cover__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Product teardown · self-initiated</p>
      <h1>Airbnb's booking flow is built for flow. <em>Then it interrupts itself.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I booked a stay in Lisbon on Airbnb's mobile site and checked each screen against
        Csikszentmihalyi's conditions for flow. Most of the journey is excellent. The friction
        comes from a handful of messages competing for attention.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="phone phone--cover" aria-label="Screens from the session, cycling: the homepage, the translation notice, the Rare find pop-up and the sticky Rare find bar">
      <div class="phone__screen phone__screen--cycle">
        <img src={img('01-arrival')} alt="" width="780" height="1328" />
        <img src={img('03-listing-translation-modal')} alt="" width="780" height="1328" />
        <img src={img('04-listing-rare-find')} alt="" width="780" height="1328" />
        <img src={img('08-sticky-rare-find')} alt="" width="780" height="1328" />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="phone__cap">Real screens from the session</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <!-- 01 Lens -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">01 · The lens</p>
    <h2 class="r">What flow asks of a product</h2>
    <p class="chapter__lede r">
      Psychologist Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi described flow as the state of being fully absorbed in
      a task. A booking is a stressful, high-stakes task: money, dates and trust in a stranger's
      home. Five of his conditions translate directly into product requirements.
    </p>
    <ol class="lens">
      <li class="r"><b>Clear goals</b><span>The guest always knows the next step.</span></li>
      <li class="r"><b>Immediate feedback</b><span>Each action shows its consequence, especially on price.</span></li>
      <li class="r"><b>Concentration</b><span>Nothing competes with the task.</span></li>
      <li class="r"><b>Sense of control</b><span>Mistakes are reversible, and commitments are explicit.</span></li>
      <li class="r"><b>No self-consciousness</b><span>No pressure or anxiety pulling the guest out of the task.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 Method -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">02 · Method</p>
    <h2 class="r">One booking, every interruption counted</h2>
    <p class="chapter__note r">
      Mobile web on an emulated iPhone, logged out, English (UK), from Lithuania. Lisbon, 3 nights, 2 adults.
      One session is a sample of one, so treat this as a set of hypotheses.
    </p>
    <ol class="path">
      {interruptions.map((s, i) => (
        <li class="path__step r">
          <p class="path__screen"><span>{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>{s.screen}</p>
          <p class:list={['path__count', { 'is-zero': s.count === 0 }]}><b>{s.count}</b> interruption{s.count === 1 ? '' : 's'}</p>
          <ul>{s.items.length ? s.items.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>) : <li class="path__clear">Clear run</li>}</ul>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="pull r">
      Five interruptions before I saw the first listing photo clearly. Search results, the part that
      needs the most concentration, had <strong>none</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 What works -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">03 · What Airbnb gets right</p>
    <h2 class="r">Four moments that protect flow</h2>
    {keeps.map((k) => (
      <div class="shot-row">
        <figure class="phone r">
          <div class="phone__screen">
            <img src={img(k.shot.file)} alt={k.shot.alt} width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
            {k.shot.pins.map((p, j) => (
              <span class="pin" style={`top:${p.top}%;left:${p.left}%;--k:${j}`} aria-hidden="true">{j + 1}</span>
            ))}
          </div>
        </figure>
        <div class="shot-row__notes r">
          <p class="eyebrow">{k.condition}</p>
          <h3>{k.title}</h3>
          <p>{k.body}</p>
          <ol class="pin-notes">
            {k.shot.pins.map((p, j) => <li style={`--k:${j}`}><span>{j + 1}</span>{p.note}</li>)}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <!-- 04 Where it breaks -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">04 · Where flow breaks</p>
    <h2 class="r">Three messages pulling the other way</h2>

    <div class="shot-row">
      <figure class="phone r">
        <div class="phone__screen">
          <img src={img('01-arrival')} alt="Airbnb mobile homepage covered by a cookie consent dialog, with a Get the app banner above" width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
          <span class="pin" style="top:10.8%;left:62%;--k:0" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <span class="pin" style="top:32%;left:91%;--k:1" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <div class="shot-row__notes r">
        <p class="eyebrow">Arrival</p>
        <h3>Two asks before the first search</h3>
        <p>
          A guest arrives with one goal, to search, and meets a banner pushing the app and a
          consent dialog. Consent is a legal requirement in the EU, so it is a constraint, not a
          choice. The app banner is a business choice, and it comes back on the listing page.
        </p>
        <ol class="pin-notes">
          <li style="--k:0"><span>1</span>"Get the app", shown again on listing pages</li>
          <li style="--k:1"><span>2</span>Cookie consent, required by law</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="shot-row shot-row--pair">
      <figure class="phone r">
        <div class="phone__screen">
          <img src={img('03-listing-translation-modal')} alt="A full-screen Translation on notice covering an Airbnb listing as it opens" width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
          <span class="pin" style="top:44%;left:50%;--k:0" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="phone r">
        <div class="phone__screen">
          <img src={img('06-inline-translation')} alt="Further down the same listing, an inline note says some info has been automatically translated, with a Show original link" width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
          <span class="pin" style="top:22.5%;left:90%;--k:0" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <div class="shot-row__notes r">
        <p class="eyebrow">Listing · first load</p>
        <h3>A full-screen notice for something already said inline</h3>
        <p>
          The first translated listing opens under a full-screen "Translation on" notice that hides
          the photos. Yet the same page already carries an inline line, "Some info has been
          automatically translated. Show original". The information is useful. The full-screen
          interruption isn't.
        </p>
        <ol class="pin-notes">
          <li style="--k:0"><span>1</span>Full-screen notice on arrival</li>
          <li style="--k:1"><span>2</span>The inline notice that already does the job</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="shot-row shot-row--pair">
      <figure class="phone r">
        <div class="phone__screen">
          <img src={img('04-listing-rare-find')} alt="Airbnb listing with a Rare find, this place is usually booked pop-up near the Reserve button" width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
          <span class="pin" style="top:79%;left:92%;--k:0" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="phone r">
        <div class="phone__screen">
          <img src={img('08-sticky-rare-find')} alt="Further down the listing, a Rare find bar stays pinned above the Reserve button while scrolling" width="780" height="1328" loading="lazy" />
          <span class="pin" style="top:83%;left:88%;--k:0" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <div class="shot-row__notes r">
        <p class="eyebrow">Listing · every scroll position</p>
        <h3>"Rare find", three times</h3>
        <p>
          A "Rare find! This place is usually booked" pop-up appears as the listing opens. Then the
          same line stays pinned above the Reserve button through the whole page, and it appears
          again in checkout. Scarcity can lift conversion. In flow terms, it adds the pressure the
          rest of the design works hard to remove.
        </p>
        <ol class="pin-notes">
          <li style="--k:0"><span>1</span>Pop-up as the page opens</li>
          <li style="--k:1"><span>2</span>Pinned bar on every scroll position, then again in checkout</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 Prioritise -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">05 · Prioritise</p>
    <h2 class="r">What to change first</h2>
    <p class="chapter__note r">Placement is my judgement from one session, not measured impact.</p>
    <div class="matrix-wrap">
      <figure class="matrix r" aria-label="Effort versus flow impact for four changes">
        <span class="matrix__y">Flow impact →</span>
        <span class="matrix__x">Effort →</span>
        {matrix.map((m, i) => (
          <span class:list={['matrix__dot', `matrix__dot--${m.kind}`]} style={`left:${m.x}%;bottom:${m.y}%;--k:${i}`}>{m.id}</span>
        ))}
      </figure>
      <ol class="matrix-key">
        {matrix.map((m) => (
          <li class="r">
            <span class:list={['matrix__dot', 'matrix__dot--static', `matrix__dot--${m.kind}`]}>{m.id}</span>
            <div>
              <b>{m.name}</b>
              <em>{{ ship: 'Ship directly', test: 'A/B test first', later: 'Needs a business case', constraint: 'Legal constraint' }[m.kind]}</em>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="reasons">
      <p class="r"><b>Ship A.</b> The inline notice already exists, so removing the full-screen version loses no information and carries little risk.</p>
      <p class="r"><b>Test B.</b> Scarcity messaging probably drives bookings, so changing it needs evidence, not opinion.</p>
      <p class="r"><b>Park C and D.</b> App installs are a business goal with their own case, and consent is required by law.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 Experiment -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">06 · The experiment</p>
    <h2 class="r">Say "Rare find" once, at the moment of decision</h2>
    <div class="variants">
      <div class="variant r">
        <p class="eyebrow">Control · today</p>
        <ul><li>Pop-up as the listing opens</li><li>Pinned bar on every scroll position</li><li>Label in checkout</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="variant variant--test r">
        <p class="eyebrow">Variant</p>
        <ul><li>No pop-up</li><li>One line beside the price when the guest scrolls to the calendar or taps Reserve</li><li>Label in checkout unchanged</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <dl class="exp">
      <div class="r"><dt>Hypothesis</dt><dd>Showing scarcity once, at the decision point, keeps bookings level while reducing regret.</dd></div>
      <div class="r"><dt>Primary metric</dt><dd>Listing view to completed booking</dd></div>
      <div class="r"><dt>Regret signal</dt><dd>Cancellations within 48 hours of booking</dd></div>
      <div class="r"><dt>Guardrail</dt><dd>Revenue per listing visitor</dd></div>
      <div class="r"><dt>Ship if</dt><dd>Bookings hold within an agreed margin and early cancellations fall. I would set the margin from a baseline first.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 Concept -->
  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <p class="chapter__num r">07 · Concept</p>
    <h2 class="r">The same listing, with one message at a time</h2>
    <div class="concept">
      <figure class="phone phone--concept r" aria-label="Concept sketch of the listing page with an inline translation label and a single scarcity line next to the price">
        <div class="phone__screen concept__screen">
          <div class="concept__photo"><span>Listing photos, unobstructed</span></div>
          <div class="concept__body">
            <span class="concept__line concept__line--title"></span>
            <span class="concept__chip">Translated · Show original</span>
            <span class="concept__line"></span><span class="concept__line concept__line--short"></span>
            <span class="concept__line"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="concept__bar">
            <div><b>€258 total</b><span class="concept__scarce">Usually booked for these dates</span></div>
            <span class="concept__btn">Reserve</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="phone__cap">My concept, not Airbnb's design</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul class="concept__notes">
        <li class="r"><b>Photos first.</b> Nothing covers the listing as it opens.</li>
        <li class="r"><b>Translation inline.</b> A small label near the description, as already exists further down.</li>
        <li class="r"><b>Scarcity once.</b> One factual line next to the price, at the moment it matters.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Credits -->
  <footer class="wrap story-credits">
    <div class="r">
      <p class="eyebrow">Limits</p>
      <p>One logged-out session on mobile web, from the EU. The app, logged-in guests and other markets may behave differently. With access, I would check how often each interruption actually appears across sessions before anything else.</p>
      <p class="story-credits__fine">Independent exercise. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Airbnb. Screenshots captured by me on 12 Nov 2026 for commentary, with hosts' and guests' names and photos blurred.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>
</article>
